                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids"} geometric quantization of symplectic groupoids
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"}> prequantum line bundle
                            ,
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+circle+n-bundle"} prequantum circle n-bundle
                          %ul
                            %li
                              %p
                                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contact+manifold"}> contact manifold
                                ,
                                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contactomorphism"} contactomorphism
                            %li
                              %p
                                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contact+form"}> contact form
                                ,
                                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reeb+vector+field"} Reeb vector field
                        %li
                          %p
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"}> quantomorphism group
                            ,
                            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+n-group"} quantomorphism n-group
                          %ul
                            %li
                              %p
                                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"}> Poisson bracket
                                ,
                                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+algebra"} Poisson algebra
                            %li
                              %p
                                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket+Lie+n-algebra"} Poisson bracket Lie n-algebra
                            %li
                              %p
                                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+Lie+algebra"}> Heisenberg Lie algebra
                                ,
                                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+Lie+n-algebra"} Heisenberg Lie n-algebra
                            %li
                              %p
                                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+group"} Heisenberg group
                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/symplectic+geometry+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#HarmonicOscillator"} Harmonic oscillator
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \omega)
            %em Bohr-Sommerfeld leaf
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+submanifold"} Lagrangian submanifold
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            on which not only the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            vanishes, but on which also a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+bundle"} prequantization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is trivializable.
          %p
            Therefore given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+polarization"} real polarization
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliation"} foliation
            by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+submanifolds"} Lagrangian submanifolds
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/leaf+space"} leaf space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Niels+Bohr"} Bohr
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arnold+Sommerfeld"} Sommerfeld
            originally considered (in less abstract terms, the archetypical example was the harmonic oscillator as discussed
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#HarmonicOscillator"} below
          %p
            (There is a correction to this picture, given by the fact that a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiclassical+states"} semiclassical states
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/half-densities"} half-densities
            over these. These are to satisfy an additional condition, encoded by the
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaplectic+correction"} metaplectic correction
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            be a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+manifold"}> pre-
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+bundle"} prequatization
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connection"} principal connection
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            2-form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mo ∇
                    %mo =
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\nabla = \omega
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+submanifold"} Lagrangian submanifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \hookrightarrow X
              is a
              %strong Bohr-Sommerfeld leaf
              if the restriction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla|_L
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+connection"} prequantum connection
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is trivializable there, hence if its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
              class vanishes in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mo ∇
                      %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ∈
                    %msubsup
                      %mi H
                      %mi conn
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    [\nabla_L] = 0 \in H^2_{conn}(X)
                    \\,.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              For every
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotropic+submanifold"} isotropic submanifold
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+submanifold"} Lagrangian submanifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \hookrightarrow X
              the restriction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla|_L
              is necessarily already a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat connection
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
              of their
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
              is trivial. Therefore a Bohr-Sommerfeld leaf is equivalently a Lagrangian submanifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla|_L
              has trivial holonomy. In this form the Bohr-Sommerfeld condition is usually stated in the literature.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              The Bohr-Sommerfeld condition is the natural lift of the
              = succeed "-condition" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+subspace"} Lagrangian subspace
            %p
              When expressed in terms of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} smooth
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stacks"} moduli stacks
              (see at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"} geometry of physics
              for background), the (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+manifold"}> pre-
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic structure
              is equivalently a map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi cl
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\omega \;\colon\; X \to \Omega^2_{cl}
            %p
              and a prequantization
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
              is equivalently a lift
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
              in the diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %msub
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mi conn
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mo ∇
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi F
                                    %mrow
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi ω
                          %mtd
                            %msubsup
                              %mi Ω
                              %mi cl
                              %mn 2
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; \mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{\nabla}}\nearrow &amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{F_{(-)}}}
                    \\\
                    X &amp;\stackrel{\omega}{\to}&amp; \Omega^2_{cl}(X)
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              The condition on an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotropic+submanifold"} isotropic submanifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \hookrightarrow X
              is that the composite map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi L
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↪
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi ω
                          %mtd
                            %msubsup
                              %mi Ω
                              %mi cl
                              %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\omega|_L
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\array{
                    L &amp;\hookrightarrow &amp;  X &amp;\stackrel{\omega}{\to}&amp; \Omega^2_{cl}
                    }
            %p
              is trivial in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo ,
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi cl
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi cl
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(L,\Omega^2_{cl}) = \Omega^2_{cl}(L)
              (and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              being Lagrangian means that it is maximal with this property). Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is Bohr-Sommerfeld if moreover the restriction of the prequantum lift
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi L
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↪
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mo ∇
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %msub
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mi conn
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\nabla|_L
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\array{
                    L &amp;\hookrightarrow &amp;  X &amp;\stackrel{\nabla}{\to}&amp; \mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}
                    }
            %p
              is trivial in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi L
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi conn
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msubsup
                        %mi H
                        %mi conn
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H(L, \mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}) = H^2_{conn}(X)
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#HarmonicOscillator Harmonic oscillator
          %p
            For the single 1-dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Harmonic+oscillator"} Harmonic oscillator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
            equipped with the symplectic form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi dR
                  %mi t
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %mi θ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\omega = d_{dR} t \wedge d\theta
                  \\,,
          %p
            where on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2- \mathbb{R}_+
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (t, \theta)
            are the canonical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polar+coordinates"} polar coordinates
          %p
            We may choose the trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+line+bundle"} prequantum line bundle
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            given by the globally defined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential 1-form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Θ
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi t
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi d
                  %mi θ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Theta := t \wedge d\theta
                  \\,.
          %p
            Then a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarization
            is given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliation"} foliation
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/leaves"} leaves
            are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifolds"} submanifolds
            of constant
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+derivative"} covariant derivative
            along any leaf acts as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mi Θ
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi θ
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi i
                  %mi t
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\nabla_\Theta \sigma)(t, \theta)
                  \= (\frac{\partial}{\partial \theta} \sigma)(t, \theta)
                  \- i t \sigma(t, \theta)
                  \\,.
          %p The covariantly sections covariantly constat on a leaf hence must be of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mi t
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\sigma(t, \theta) = a(t) \exp( i t \theta)
                  \\,.
          %p For this to be well-defined as a globally defined section on the whole leaf the condition
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                  %mi k
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi k
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  t = 2 \pi k \; \; k \in \mathbb{Z}
          %p
            has to hold. Hence the Bohr-Sommerfeld leaves here are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circles"} circles
            of radius
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \pi k
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
          %h2#properties Properties
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Guillemin-Sternberg)
            %p
              If a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarization
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+fibration"} regular fibration
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/leaves"} leaves
              over a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected"} simply connected
              base
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi BS
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\{F_BS\} = \{
                    p \in X | (f_1(p), \cdots, f_n(p)) \in \mathbb{Z}^n
                    \\}
            %p
              where the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{f_i\}
              are global
              %span.newWikiWord
                action coordinates
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/action+coordinates"} ?
              on the base space
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Sniatycki)
            %p
              If the leaf space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff
              and the projection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to B
              has
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
              fibers, then the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+states+%28in+geometric+quantization%29"} space of quantum states
              is given by the number of Bohr-Sommerfeld leaves.
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Named after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Niels+Bohr"} Niels Bohr
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arnold+Sommerfeld"} Arnold Sommerfeld
          %p For historical background see also
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_quantum_theory"} Old quantum theory
          %p
            Discussion in the modern formalism of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
          %ul
            %li{:id => "Śniatycki75"}
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C4%99drzej+%C5%9Aniatycki"}> Jędrzej Śniatycki
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Wave functions relative to a real polarization
                %strong 14
                4 (1975) 277-288 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01807689"}> doi:10.1007/BF01807689)
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li{:id => "Śniatycki80"}
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C4%99drzej+%C5%9Aniatycki"}> Jędrzej Śniatycki
                ,
